===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wrenǵʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|wringen}}, {{m|enm|wryngen|t=to press, or twist and squeeze, (something) to extract liquid; to drain, strain; to dry off (something); of a liquid: to flow as if squeezed out; to press, squeeze, twist, or wrench (something); to hold on to (something) greedily; of a spear shaft: to twist while moving and miss the target, veer; of hands: to press and twist together as a sign of distress, sorrow, etc.; of a person: to squirm, writhe; to torment mentally or physically; to escape; to uproot}}{{nb...|ringe, wreng, wring, wringe, wringge, wringin, wryng, wrynge, wryngene, wryngyn|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=wringen|pos=v|id=MED53673}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|wringan|t=to wring}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=230711|date=December 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref> from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wringaną|t=to squeeze, twist, wring}}, possibly from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*wrenǵʰ-}}.

