# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{senseid|en|squeeze}} ''Often followed by'' '''[[out#Preposition|out]]''': to [[squeeze#Verb|squeeze]] or [[twist#Verb|twist]] (something [[moist#Adjective|moist]]) [[tightly]] so that [[liquid#Noun|liquid]] is [[force out|forced out]].
##: {{ux|en|I didn’t have a towel so I just '''wrung''' my hair dry.}}
##* {{RQ:Lyly Euphues|page=325|passage={{...}} ''[[w:Protogenes|Protagenes]]'' portrai[e]d ''[[w:Venus (mythology)|Venus]]'' with a ſponge ſprinkled with ſweete water, but if once ſhe '''wrong''' it, it would drop bloud: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Poe Pym|chapter=XIII|pages=122–123|pageref=123|passage=[W]e contrived to satisfy the cravings of thirst by suffering the shirts to become saturated, and then '''wringing''' them so as to let the grateful fluid trickle into our mouths.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Down and Out|chapter=XXI|page=154|passage={{...}} he had sometimes '''wrung''' a dirty dishcloth into a customer’s soup before taking it in, just to be revenged upon a member of the bourgeoisie.}}
### {{senseid|en|wringer}} To squeeze [[water#Noun|water]] from (an [[item#Noun|item]] of [[wet#Adjective|wet]] [[clothing#Noun|clothing]]) by [[pass#Verb|passing]] through a [[wringer]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Anne Tyler|authorlink=Anne Tyler|chapter=1|title={{w|Breathing Lessons}}|series=A Borzoi Book|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Alfred A. Knopf|Alfred A[braham] Knopf]]|year=1988|section=part 1|page=25|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/breathinglessonstyle00tyle/page/25/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-394-57234-5|passage=“I feel I’ve been '''wrung''' through a wringer,” Maggie said.}}
## {{lb|en|also|figuratively}} ''Often followed by'' '''[[from]]''' ''or'' '''out''': to [[extract#Verb|extract]] (a liquid) from something wet by squeezing, twisting, or otherwise putting [[pressure#Noun|pressure]] on it.
##: {{ux|en|Put the berries into a cheesecloth and '''wring''' the juice into a bowl.}}
##* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|Judges|6|38|folio=xvi|column=1|passage=And whan he roſe vp early on the morow, he '''wrãge''' [wrange] y&#868; dew out of the [[fleece|fleſe]], and fylled a dyſſhe full of water.}}
##* {{RQ:Evelyn Diary|volume=I|entrydate=21 May 1645|page=178|passage=At the end of this very long walk stands a woman in white marble, in posture of a laundress '''wringing''' water out of a piece of linen, very naturally formed, into a vast lavor the work and invention of [[w:Michelangelo|M[ichel] Angelo Buonarotti]].}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Roderick Random|volume=I|chapter=XIV|page=107|passage=[He] '''wrung''' the urine out of his perriwig, and lifting up a large stone, flung it with such force against the street-door of that house from whence he had been bedewed, that the lock giving way, it flew wide open,}}
##* {{RQ:Ruskin Political Economy of Art|page=17|passage=[Y]ou have to dig the moor and dry the marsh, to bid the morass give forth instead of engulphing, and to '''wring''' the honey and oil out of the rock.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Zora Neale Hurston|authorlink=Zora Neale Hurston|chapter=Backstage and the Railroad|editor={{w|William Loren Katz}}|title={{w|Dust Tracks on a Road}}|series=The American Negro: His History and Literature|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Arno Press}} and {{w|The New York Times}}|year=1952|year_published=1969|page=128|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dusttracksonroad00hurs/page/128/mode/1up|oclc=88431|passage=Heinz could have '''wrung''' enough vinegar out of Cally’s look to run his pickle works.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=John Irving|authorlink=John Irving|chapter=The Finger|title=[[w:A Prayer for Owen Meany|A Prayer for Owen Meany{{nb...|A Novel}}]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|William Morrow and Company}}|year=1989|page=381|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/prayerforowenmeairv00irvi/page/381/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-688-07708-2|passage={{...}} I thought that he was as pleased by the shock value of what he had to say as he was thrilled by the spectacle of '''wringing''' his own blood from the sodden gauze pad into the sodden towel.}}
