##: {{ux|en|to '''wring''' the neck of a chicken}}
##* {{RQ:King James Version|Leviticus|1|15|page=184|column=1|passage=And the Prieſt ſhall bring it [a dove] vnto the altar, and '''wring''' off his head, and burne it on the altar: {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author={{w|Elizabeth Gaskell}}|title={{w|North and South (Gaskell novel)|North and South}}|location=London|publisher=Chapman & Hall|volume=1|chapter=16|page=195|url=https://archive.org/details/northsouth01gask/page/194|passage=Margaret could not speak for crying; but she '''wrung''' his hand at parting.}}
##* {{RQ:Montgomery Anne of the Island|chapter=A Book of Revelation|page=316|passage=The Haunted Wood was full of the groans of mighty trees '''wrung''' in the tempest, and the air throbbed with the thunderous crash of billows on the distant shore.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Elizabeth Metzger Howard|chapter=2|title=Before the Sun Goes Down|location=Garden City, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday & Company]]|year=1946|section=part I (Summer)|page=31|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/beforesungoesdow00howa/page/31/mode/1up|oclc=657021|passage=Jesus Christ! Was my folks refined. My mam she wouldn't think-a lettin' us young'uns call a pee pot a pee pot. A chamber's what she called it. {{...}} And by God! Us young'uns had ter call the pee pot a chamber or git our God damn necks '''wrang'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Faulkner Sound and Fury|chapter=April 8, 1928|page=320|passage=Jason stood, slowly '''wringing''' the brim of his hat in his hands.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Aravind Adiga|authorlink=Aravind Adiga|title={{w|The White Tiger}}|edition=1st UK|location=London|publisher={{w|Atlantic Books}}|year=2008|page=262|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781843547204/page/262/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-84354-720-4|passage={{...}} I had to '''wring''' your ears to make you do any work.}}
